\section{Additional Observations}
\label{app:additional}

\textbf{Per-task concentration.} Seven of ten tasks achieve CAR $\geq$ 0.92 under Vanilla; overall CAR is dominated by three physically constrained tasks (T3, T5, T6; Section~\ref{app:per_task}). Detach is systematically worst on these hard tasks (T3: 0.11, T5: 0.05), consistent with reduced gradient flow exacerbating degradation where obstacle proximity is tight.

\textbf{Egocentric depth matches Vanilla.} Depth (4 seeds) achieves CAR 0.780 $\pm$ 0.039, identical to Vanilla, despite providing per-pixel geometric information. Three label types (structured BEV, random binary, egocentric depth) yield comparable CAR when gradient flow is maintained.

\textbf{Temporal dynamics (preliminary).} Cutoff@35K (3 seeds) achieves CAR 0.779, statistically indistinguishable from Vanilla ($\Delta = -$0.1\,pp), which confirms that 35K steps of gradient flow fully protects collision avoidance. Surprisingly, Cutoff@25K (0.724, 2 seeds) and Reverse Cutoff (0.718, 2 seeds) fall substantially \emph{below} Vanilla. The most parsimonious explanation: setting the loss weight to zero retains the auxiliary head forward pass, so the post-cutoff period operates in Detach mode; BatchNorm interference acts on representations shaped by gradient flow, without gradient compensation. This is not withdrawal from gradient flow \emph{per se}, but transition into an actively harmful regime. Third seeds are in progress; extended analysis in Section~\ref{app:temporal}.

\section{Limitations}
\label{app:limitations}

Our study uses a single architecture (OpenVLA-OFT with LoRA rank 32); full fine-tuning may alter the interaction between auxiliary heads and encoder optimization because all parameters update simultaneously, potentially changing the gradient flow dynamics we observe. All experiments are conducted in MuJoCo simulation (SafeLIBERO Level~I, 10 tasks); real-world deployment introduces additional sources of distributional shift that could interact differently with auxiliary head training dynamics. The benchmark contains only static obstacles; dynamic obstacle scenarios may induce time-varying distributions where auxiliary head dynamics behave differently. Our collision metric is binary (L1 displacement threshold $10^{-3}$), which does not capture collision severity or near-miss behavior; a continuous safety metric might reveal finer-grained differences between conditions. CKA measures linear representational similarity, which may miss nonlinear structure relevant to safety behavior. The ordinal interaction is based on $n{=}1$--$4$ seeds per cell; larger-scale replication would further strengthen conclusions. Cutoff conditions have 1--3 seeds, limiting temporal conclusions to preliminary observations.
